--[[
    Fix HD Inventory Icon Position: restore one decimal place on carry-weight
    stats in item tooltips, dropped by the HD framework's copy of utils_ui.script.

    Why this exists:
      Sota's utils_ui.script forces the tooltip's decimal-place setting to 1 for
      the two carry-weight stats, in the stat-row branch of UIInfoItem:Update:

          -- fix rounding accuracy for weight property
          if (stat == "additional_inventory_weight") or (stat == "boost_max_weight") then
              idp_stats_tt = 1

      The HD framework ships an older fork of that file (2025-03-27 vs Sota's
      2025-09-22) and wins the load-order conflict, so the line is gone and the
      stats fall back to the MCM default, which is 0.

      ui_inventory.stats_round_idp already protects small numbers -- under 1 it
      raises the decimal count itself -- so only values of 1 kg and up flatten.
      Across base Anomaly's 201 non-zero additional_inventory_weight values, 31
      lose precision, by up to 0.5 kg (1.58 shows as 2, 2.58 as 3). GAMMA's
      artefact and backpack mods add more. Since these are numbers players read
      to compare outfits and backpacks, being half a kilo out matters.

    Why this needs the unlocalizer:
      idp_stats_tt is a function-local inside UIInfoItem:Update, so there is no
      seam to hook -- the engine's unlocalizer only reaches column-0 file-scope
      declarations. What it can reach is line 41, which caches the rounding
      function itself. Once that is promoted out of file scope we can wrap the
      rounding call instead of the setting that feeds it, and force the decimal
      place from there.

      Knowing which stat is being rounded takes one more hook: get_stats_value
      is called once per stat at the top of each iteration of the stat loop
      (utils_ui.script:2824), and every stats_round_idp call in that iteration
      follows it with the same stat. get_stats_string_value has the same shape
      (call at :423, rounding at :435 and :439). So recording the stat there
      covers all four rounding sites.

      This assumes get_stats_value is not re-entrant. Its value_functors call
      into other modules but none call back into it, so the recorded stat cannot
      be clobbered mid-iteration.

    Why the usual fragility argument does not apply:
      Unlocalizing normally risks silently no-opping if upstream reformats the
      declaration. The file being rewritten here belongs to an unmaintained mod,
      so it will not change -- and Sota's copy has the identical line at the same
      column, so this still lands if the load order ever flips. In that case
      Sota's own code sets the same decimal place and our wrapper becomes a no-op
      rather than a conflict.

    If the unlocalizer did not apply (no modded exes, or the config did not
    load) then utils_ui.stats_round_idp is nil, and we log once and do nothing.
--]]



local WEIGHT_STATS = {
    ["additional_inventory_weight"] = true,
    ["boost_max_weight"]            = true,
}

-- Stat currently being rendered, recorded by the get_stats_value wrapper and
-- read by the stats_round_idp wrapper later in the same loop iteration.
local current_stat = nil


function on_game_start()
    if not (utils_ui and utils_ui.get_stats_value) then
        return
    end

    -- Present only if the unlocalizer promoted it out of file scope.
    if type(utils_ui.stats_round_idp) ~= "function" then
        printf("! FIXHD| weight rounding fix inactive: utils_ui.stats_round_idp is not exposed "
            .. "(unlocalizer_fix_hd_stats_rounding.ltx did not apply -- modded exes required)")
        return
    end

    local base_get_stats_value = utils_ui.get_stats_value
    utils_ui.get_stats_value = function(obj, sec, gr, stat)
        current_stat = stat
        return base_get_stats_value(obj, sec, gr, stat)
    end

    local base_stats_round_idp = utils_ui.stats_round_idp
    utils_ui.stats_round_idp = function(num, idp)
        if current_stat and WEIGHT_STATS[current_stat] and ((idp or 0) < 1) then
            idp = 1
        end
        return base_stats_round_idp(num, idp)
    end
end
